cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "elenctic: a declarative testing framework for Answer Set Programming"
message: "If you use elenctic in academic work, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Gelfond
    given-names: Gregory
repository-code: "https://github.com/GregoryGelfond/elenctic"
license: MIT
# Single-sourced from `elenctic.__version__`, and checked against it — a citation naming a release
# that was never cut is worse than one naming none.
version: 0.3.0
abstract: >-
  elenctic checks Answer Set Programming encodings against contracts written as
  in-file annotations, in the program's own reasoning modes: what holds in every
  answer set (cautious), what holds in some (brave), what is optimal, how many
  solutions there are, and what the program answers to a three-valued epistemic
  query (yes / no / unknown, after Gelfond and Kahl). Verdicts are three-valued,
  so a search that could not decide is never reported as a program that decided
  wrongly.
keywords:
  - answer set programming
  - knowledge representation
  - logic programming
  - software testing
  - clingo
